nexus_design_workflow.phase:
Motion is required, and it is more than one fade. The motion layer has five parts: **two or three scroll scenes** (the big, scroll-driven moments: a camera flying through a field of pictures, a picture that grows from a window to the whole screen, type that crosses a held stage, cards that deal out and stack — the direction sheet deals them from `library/scenes.json`; `references/scenes.md` says how to fill and fit them); **one signature moment** (the thing a visitor would describe an hour later — the direction names one; the only move allowed to be loud); **section entrances, choreographed per section** (the parts of each section arrive in reading order, staggered 60–90 ms, rising 16–32 px with opacity, once; vary the manner with the content); **feedback under the hand** (press, hover, focus, menus opening from where they were clicked); and **one moving picture** (a found clip as a muted loop with a poster, a slow drift on a photograph, or a scroll-linked move on the hero). What is still banned: the same fade-and-slide-up on every block; motion that hides content until a script runs with no fallback; bounce and elastic easing; layout-property animation.
6.1 Tool selection matrix. UI enter/exit/layout → Framer Motion (AnimatePresence, layoutId, springs). Scroll storytelling (pin, scrub) → GSAP + ScrollTrigger (frame-accurate control), built on the shared stage in `assets/scenes/stage.js` (load order and pinned CDN lines in its header comment). Looping icons → Lottie (lazy-load, ~50 KB). 3D/WebGL → Three.js / R3F (isolated, own client boundary). Hover/focus states → CSS only (zero JS cost). Native scroll-driven → `animation-timeline: scroll()` / `view()`, inside `@supports`, static without it.
Conflict rules: never mix GSAP and Framer Motion in the same component. R3F must live in an isolated Canvas wrapper. Always lazy-load Lottie, GSAP, and Three.js. Wheel capture, custom cursors and preloaders are banned; smoothing only through `Stage.smooth()` (Lenis at lerp 0.12, off for touch and reduced motion).
6.2 Intensity scale. 1–2 Subtle CSS transitions only, 150–300 ms. 3–4 Smooth CSS keyframes + Framer animate, stagger ≤3 items. 5–6 Fluid whileInView, magnetic hover, parallax tilt. 7–8 Cinematic GSAP ScrollTrigger, pinned sections, horizontal hijack. 9–10 Immersive full scroll sequences, Three.js particles, WebGL shaders. The inspector fails a page whose load + one scroll moves fewer than 8 distinct elements; warns under 16.
6.3 Performance rules. Animate GPU-only properties: transform, opacity, filter, clip-path. Never animate width, height, top, left, margin, padding, or font-size — use transform: scale() or clip-path instead. Perpetual animations must be in React.memo leaf components. Use will-change: transform only during animation. Use contain: layout style paint on heavy containers. Respect prefers-reduced-motion always. Disable parallax and 3D on pointer: coarse. Cap particles: desktop 800, tablet 300, mobile 100. Disable GSAP pin on mobile below 768 px. Every useEffect with GSAP or observers must return a cleanup: () => ctx.revert(). Do not drive many children from one changing CSS variable on a parent; set the transform on the element that moves.
6.4 Motion accessibility. Wrap motion in prefers-reduced-motion checks; the reduced page is the finished page — every hidden state lives inside `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)`. Never flash content more than three times per second (seizure risk). Provide visible focus rings using outline, not box-shadow. Add aria-live="polite" for dynamically revealed content. Include a pause button for auto-playing animations. Never leave content at `opacity: 0` waiting for a script that may fail (the `html.js` gate handles no-JS).
6.5 Motion tokens (October 2026 study of 99 Sites of the Day):
```css
:root {
  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);        /* entrances, moving hovers, UI under 600ms */
  --ease-expo: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);        /* reveals of 800ms and longer, line rises, openings */
  --ease-in-out: cubic-bezier(0.76, 0, 0.24, 1);     /* wipes, panels, things that travel while visible */
  --ease-soft: cubic-bezier(0.33, 1, 0.68, 1);       /* slow scale and drift on photographs */
  --ease-exit: cubic-bezier(0.55, 0.055, 0.675, 0.19); /* something leaving for good, short */
  --t-press: 140ms;
  --t-hover: 180ms;
  --t-ui: 240ms;
  --t-reveal: 800ms;
  --t-open: 1200ms;                                  /* picture openings and clip wipes */
  --stagger-line: 90ms;
  --stagger-item: 70ms;
}

Durations by job: press 140 ms; hover colour 180–200 ms; hover that moves 300–400 ms; menus 240 ms; reveals 700–900 ms; picture openings 1,100–1,300 ms; exits 60–70% of the matching entrance. Staggers: lines 80–110 ms; list items 50–90 ms; characters 20–40 ms and only in a heading of one to three words; four steps at most. Curves: --ease-expo for reveals 800 ms or more (the strongest ease-outs in the study), --ease-out for anything under 600 ms, --ease-in-out for wipes and travel, --ease-exit only for things leaving for good, linear for scroll-linked motion and loops, plain ease for colour. Overshoot stays banned on interface elements.
